<p>There's an old quote which is something like "do you work to live, or do you live to work"?</p>
<p>I have spoken elsewhere about the utter pointlessness of life, and that it's really up to each of us to find the things in life that add value and meaning; there is no external compass or guide.  And one of the easy cop outs is to say "my work is my life".  It stops you peeling off that layer of the onion and seeing what's happening underneath.</p>
<p>So what happens to people who stop working, whether through retirement, ill health or simply not being wanted?  They can no longer use this excuse.</p>
<p>It seems to me that this opens up a grey area, which an increasing number of people are being "put in", which is that, essentially, they are waiting to die.  In some ways, this is something which has been known about for ages, and I believe is the subtext of Samuel Beckett's play  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waiting_for_Godot'>"Waiting for Godot"</a>, but let's face it, I'm not really a theatre critic.</p>
<p>But there are people who are undeniably in this category: most obviously, people in comas on life support, but then people who are just on life support, to people in nursing homes, people in social care; the list goes on.</p>
<p>Interestingly, it is <i>not</i> the same as the list of people who have crippling or debilitating diseases: there is definitely a considerable overlap, but I would offer Steven Hawking as a blindingly obvious counterexample.  On the other hand, there are many people who have "nothing physically wrong with them", who are, essentially, waiting to die.</p>
<p>Don't get me wrong: there is absolutely no judgment in anything I am writing here.  It would clearly be inconsistent to say that there is no external framework for judging life, and then to judge people for not being good enough at it.  For all I know, if I look at someone and think "they have nothing going on in their life" I could find that on closer examination they are more fulfilled in their life than I could ever be.  But how would I even tell?</p>
<p>So, what categorization <i>am</i> I using here?</p>
<p>It obviously has to be internal to the individual.  Only an individual can make that determination, which is why it is so hard for anyone else to make the determination of whether to turn off life support.</p>
<p>It obviously isn't <i>directly</i> connected to money.  Because of the societies in which many of us live, it may be the case that money is the limiting factor in accessing treatments that would physically preserve us.</p>
<p>For people who are conscious, lucid and rational, the obvious test is to ask them the question at the top of the page: do you feel that you are living your life, or just waiting to die?  There are a number of other complementary questions, any or all of which may help elucidate the main one: What do you look forward to on a daily basis?  Why do you get up in the morning?  What will the world miss about you when you're gone?</p>
<p>Again, I'm not going to say there are "right" or "wrong" answers.  I just want to be clear, precise and honest about these things.</p>
<p>I read news stories that tell me that in the UK 50% of government spending is going to go on health care and social care.  And a disproportionate amount of that goes on people at the end of their lives (especially keeping them alive for the last six months or so).  Is that worth it?  While I'm not qualified to answer that, given that "we" are collectively providing that money, it's a reasonable conversation for us to have as a society.  Do we want to spend that money to keep people alive so that they can carrying on waiting to die?  Do we want to apply some other test?  How much of it is just that people are afraid to die, not realizing that it is, of course, inevitable sooner or later?</p>
<p>It's interesting how little people are usually willing to wait for anything: top daily complaints include things like waiting in traffic; waiting for things to be delivered; waiting for books or music to be released.  Why is dying any different?</p>
